// tests/corpus/loop-nested/while_cond_nested_breaks_inner.zen
// a cond-form loop nested inside a ranged loop: the break must stop
// the WHILE loop only, and the ranged loop must keep counting.
//
// The cond form is the one overload whose body is guarded by a
// condition re-tested BEFORE every pass, so it has a second way to
// stop besides break — and the interesting failure is the two being
// conflated: lower break as "skip to the condition test" and an
// always-true guard never lets go, or as "run the condition once and
// fall out" and the wrong frame unwinds. Both wrong lowerings print
// numbers; only WHICH numbers tells them apart.
//
// The inner while is written so its condition alone would never stop
// it — `() bool { true }` — which makes the break, not the guard,
// the only thing this test observes. The counter proves the body ran
// four times per outer pass, and the outer tail line proves the exit
// stopped at the right depth.

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    Range(0, 3).loop((h_outer, outer) {
        count ::= 0;
        loop(() bool { true }, (h_while) {
            count = count + 1;
            (count == 4).then(() { h_while.break() });
            println("  while {} {}", outer, count);
        });
        println("outer {}", outer);
    });
    Ok(0);
}
